Here's everything you need for a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport:

---

## Program.cs

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("MyApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## Tool Class (e.g. `ApiTools.cs`)

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetches a user profile by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The unique user ID")] string userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("MyApi");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Searches users by name. Returns JSON array.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Search query string")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("MyApi");
        var response = await client.GetAsync(
            $"/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}",
            cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | How it works |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Services like `IHttpClientFactory`, `ILogger<T>` are injected via primary constructor. The class is resolved from the DI container per invocation. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool inputs. |

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically distinguishes DI services from tool parameters. Only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the tool's JSON schema exposed to LLMs. Types like `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service are injected silently.

Registration in `Program.cs`:
- `builder.Services.AddHttpClient(...)` → makes `IHttpClientFactory` / `HttpClient` available
- `ILogger<T>` is available by default (no registration needed)
- `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection

---

**To scaffold this quickly:**
```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
# or manually:
dotnet new console -n MyApiServer && cd MyApiServer
dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol
```
